WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
Branching is how a workflow can split into
different paths based on the result of a

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.460
step. Say one step checks an incoming
offer. If it's over a certain amount, the

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.540
workflow routes it for review, and if not,
it just continues on. In Sim, that split

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:20.080
can come from a simple rule you set,
written code, or be handed off to an agent

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.800
to decide. Let me show you how it works.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.720
A workflow doesn't have to be a straight
line. It can fork. The fork is one block,

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.120
and every branch row on it is its own
output port. A condition block picks the

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.580
branch with a rule. The priority field
resolves to high, the check passes

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.600
instantly with no model involved, and the
workflow takes the top lane. The bottom

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.100
lane never runs. Branches are checked from
top to bottom, and the first one that's

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.540
true wins. This ticket matches nothing
above it, so the else branch fires

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.740
instead. When the rule is too hard to
write, when the decision depends on

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:02.340
meaning, you swap in a router. It takes a
context to read, a model to read it, and a

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.340
set of named routes.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:09.500
Now a model makes the choice. The router
reads the ticket, thinks for a moment, and

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.980
that pause is a real model call, then it
picks the support route.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.360
The run's log shows five blocks, but only
three rows ran. The branch that wasn't

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.180
taken, never executed at all. It left no
log entry and no output. It's still one

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.960
workflow. Reach for a rule when you can
write one, and reach for a model when you

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:31.960
can't.
